<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How Do I Find a Book in the Library?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication_1: Facilitate a discussion on a group project. communication_2: Relay complex information from teachers to students.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion on a group project, using simple questions and visuals (e.g., holds up a checklist and says, 'What's next on the list?'). Can relay complex teacher instructions by breaking them into simple parts with visuals (e.g., writes 'sort books' and points to the correct shelf). Can guide classmates in completing project tasks with written steps and gestures (e.g., writing 'return books' and gestures putting books on a shelf).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying a simple, labeled picture of a library section (e.g., "Fiction Books," "Reference Section"). Students are asked to recall vocabulary. "What can you find in this section?" "Should we be quiet here?" "How many books are on this shelf?" This activates previous knowledge and vocabulary like <i>section, shelf, many, quiet</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explains a simple instruction, modeling clear, step-by-step communication. "Today, we will learn how to help others understand things better. I will show you. For example, if I want you to 'take out your books, open to page 10, and read the summary,' I can break it down: <i>First, take out your books. Next, open to page 10. Then, read the summary.</i> I am mediating the instruction. Notice my clear voice and gestures. I will also model asking a yes/no question with correct intonation: 'Can I help you with this book?' (raising tone at the end).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I asking you to write a story or to explain steps clearly?" (Expected answer: To explain steps clearly.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'First, take out your books,' am I giving you one big instruction or simple parts?" (Expected answer: Simple parts.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "In the Library".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, simple dialogue between a librarian and a new student, or a project outline for the "Digital Library Map" activity that includes slightly complex instructions. For example, a poster with "Steps to find a book for your research project:" written with some detail, then below, a simplified version. The input could include: "You should go to the main catalog. Then, you can search for the author or title. Remember, many fiction books are on the first floor. Most non-fiction books are on the second floor. After you find your book, you should check it out."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Relay complex information from teachers to students; Facilitate a discussion on a group project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue/read the project outline.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main topic of these instructions?" "Who is helping whom?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher provides sentences and students identify if they are "Original Instruction" or "Simplified Step." E.g., "Locate the non-fiction area on the upper level." (Original) vs. "Go upstairs for non-fiction." (Simplified).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify key vocabulary words such as <i>catalog, author, title, section, fiction, non-fiction, shelf, research</i>.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Past simple tense (e.g., 'I liked the story.'), Modals <i>can/should</i> (e.g., 'Can I borrow this book?' 'You should be quiet.'), Imperatives (e.g., 'Go to the library catalog.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> <i>library, book, catalog, author, title, section, summary, librarian, checkout, research, outline, plan, fiction, non-fiction, shelf, many, all, most.</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice intonation for yes/no questions using modals ('Can I take out this book?') and identifying short /ɪ/ in 'pick' and long /aɪ/ in 'find'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Listing information collaboratively ('Let's make a list of books to check out.'), asking for/giving information ('Can I borrow this book?'), making polite requests ('You should be quiet.').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled practice (Simplify the Librarian's Note):</b> The teacher provides 2-3 slightly complex "librarian's notes" on the board (e.g., "All users must return borrowed materials to the designated return area prior to exiting the facility."). In pairs, students rewrite each note into 2-3 simple, clear imperative sentences or bullet points. E.g., "1. Return books here. 2. Then, you can go." Teacher circulates, checking for use of imperatives and clarity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled practice (Explaining a Task):</b> Students work in groups of three. The teacher gives each group a card with a project task (e.g., "Find information about the author of your favorite fiction book using the library catalog."). One student (A) explains the task to student (B) using simple language and gestures. Student (B) asks one "Can I...?" question (e.g., "Can I use the computer?"). Student (C) listens and gives feedback on clarity and pronunciation, focusing on the intonation of the question. Students rotate roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair interaction (Mediating Project Steps):</b> Students are given a mixed-up list of 4-5 steps for finding a book and conducting basic research (e.g., 'go to section', 'search catalog', 'read summary', 'check out book'). In pairs, they must put the steps in the correct order and then *explain* the process to each other as if one is a junior librarian helping a new student. They should use phrases like, "First, you should...", "Next, you can...", "Remember to...".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "In the Library".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Digital Library Map Presentation & Guided Tour (Group Mediation)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their project groups (from previous lessons) to present their "Digital Library Map." Each student takes a turn acting as a "tour guide" (mediator) for a different section of their digital map to the other group members (who act as new students). The goal is to explain the map and guide classmates through how to use it to find books or resources.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">During their turn, students must:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitate a discussion:</b> Use simple questions like, "What's next on our map?" or "Where can you find fiction books?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Relay information clearly:</b> Explain their map's sections (e.g., "This section has <i>many</i> non-fiction books," "<i>All</i> of these shelves are for research," "<i>Most</i> of the best stories are here.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guide with steps and gestures:</b> Demonstrate how to find something on their map (e.g., "You should go to the 'Catalog' icon," "Can you see the 'Children's Section' here?"). They can use visuals (their map) and gestures (pointing, miming searching). For example, if explaining to return books, they could say, "You should <i>return books</i> here," and gesture putting a book on a shelf.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use modals (<i>can, should</i>) and imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integrate vocabulary: <i>library, map, section, shelf, book, catalog, find, search, many, all, most</i>.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students, in their project groups, present their "Digital Library Map" and collaboratively explain how to navigate it, clearly mediating information to their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitates discussion using simple questions (e.g., "What's next?"). (Yes/No/Partial)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Relays information by breaking it into simple parts. (Yes/No/Partial)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Guides classmates with clear steps and gestures. (Yes/No/Partial)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses appropriate target vocabulary (e.g., <i>catalog, section, shelf, many, all, most</i>). (Yes/No/Partial)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modals (<i>can, should</i>) and imperatives correctly. (Yes/No/Partial)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is intelligible, especially for questions. (Yes/No/Partial)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I explained clearly today was ________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To help my classmates understand better next time, I can ________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did helping your group members understand the 'Digital Library Map' make you feel?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of your explanation do you think was most helpful for your classmates?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? (e.g., Students effectively broke down instructions, used visuals.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to better facilitate discussions or relay complex information in future lessons? (e.g., More explicit practice with asking follow-up questions, providing sentence starters for explanations.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "How Do I Find a Book in the Library?". The mediation skills practiced today are essential for collaborative projects and peer learning in any subject. Students can apply these skills to explain complex topics or instructions in future English lessons and other subjects.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>